{"name":"Ski White","hex":"#d2e3e5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ski","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Sika."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of a pair of long flat runners designed for gliding over snow or water.","One of a pair of long flat runners under some flying machines, used for landing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ski"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ski","blocks":[{"text":"Skis are runners that are designed to glide over snow. Skiers employ them in pairs, attached to ski boots with ski bindings, with either a free, lockable, or partially secured heel. For climbing slopes, ski skins can be affixed at the base of each ski to prevent them from sliding backwards. Originally used as a means of travel over snow, skis have become specialized for recreational and competitive alpine and cross-country skiing. Skis may also be attached to vehicles, such as aircraft or snowmobiles."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ski"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Ski.jpg/120px-Ski.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Ski.jpg/250px-Ski.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Ski.jpg/120px-Ski.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" A shaped alpine ski, showing sidecut—the narrowing of the ski, near the binding.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASki.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Prehistoric_and_Medieval_Skis_from_Glaciers_and_Ice_Patches_in_Norway_Fig_4_Digervarden_ski_at_the_find_spot.png/500px-Prehistoric_and_Medieval_Skis_from_Glaciers_and_Ice_Patches_in_Norway_Fig_4_Digervarden_ski_at_the_find_spot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Prehistoric_and_Medieval_Skis_from_Glaciers_and_Ice_Patches_in_Norway_Fig_4_Digervarden_ski_at_the_find_spot.png/1280px-Prehistoric_and_Medieval_Skis_from_Glaciers_and_Ice_Patches_in_Norway_Fig_4_Digervarden_ski_at_the_find_spot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Prehistoric_and_Medieval_Skis_from_Glaciers_and_Ice_Patches_in_Norway_Fig_4_Digervarden_ski_at_the_find_spot.png/500px-Prehistoric_and_Medieval_Skis_from_Glaciers_and_Ice_Patches_in_Norway_Fig_4_Digervarden_ski_at_the_find_spot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Archaeologist holding a 1,300 year-old ski